================================================================================
TEST T33248 -- IPv4_ARP - Static ARP
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-15 18:18-18:32 NZDT (the DUT's clock reads UTC: `show clock` gave
          05:16:40 UTC bracketed by the box's 05:16:43 and 05:16:49, so swi_d = box
          clock - 3 s. Every device timestamp below is swi_d's own UTC.)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2 = swi_d (DUT); Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     T33248 "IPv4_ARP - Static ARP". No authored step text exists for this case,
          in AWPTCM or in ck.db: an exact-title lookup found step text for one other
          case in this campaign and none for this one, so there are no authored steps
          and no authored expected results. The headings below say what was actually
          exercised. The case was graded against:
            - X960 platform support TPS row 20 "Static ARP", priority 1, "Yes", note
              column empty -- a row marked Yes whose command is absent would be a
              finding, not an UNSUPPORTED;
            - the IP Feature Overview and Configuration Guide, static ARP and
              "deleting ARP entries" sections;
            - the DUT's own CLI surface, read off by a read-only `?` probe (quoted in
              STEP 1), which gives the full syntax
              `arp A.B.C.D HHHH.HHHH.HHHH [IFRANGE] [alias]`.
          Objective: a static ARP entry is configurable, is held as type `static`, is
          USED by the forwarding path (the DUT stops ARPing for the address and
          rewrites the destination MAC of routed frames to the configured one), is
          removable, and survives a reboot. 5 steps as executed, plus a baseline.
Build:    swi_d  sin_x96056-20261012-24  (flash:/x960-tb277.rel, build date Mon Oct 12 2026)
          AT-x960-56YSQ, S/N A2WQS700B, standalone, console u2 @115200
Configs:  swi_d.cfg (running-config after setup, before step 1; saved to startup: no)

VERDICT: PASS -- a static ARP entry is accepted, held as `static`, and proven ON THE
WIRE to be used by the forwarding path, while an address with no entry in the same leg
is still ARPed for and forwards nothing; removal returns the DUT to the baseline in the
configuration, in the table and in the dataplane; and the entry survives a reboot with
the forwarding behaviour intact.
      baseline, no entry ................... 0 of 5 frames forwarded, 3 broadcast ARP
                                             requests for the target
      entry accepted ....................... no `% ` line; verbatim in running-config
      entry typed `static` ................. `show arp`: vlan48, port1.0.48, static
      entry used by the dataplane .......... 5 of 5 routed frames egressed on
                                             port1.0.48 with dst MAC 00:11:22:33:44:99
      DUT stops ARPing for the address ..... 3 requests in the baseline, 0 with the entry
      control in the SAME leg .............. 192.168.48.10 (no entry) still ARPed for,
                                             0 of 5 forwarded
      `alias` option, dependency ........... refused unless `ip proxy-arp` is on the
                                             owning interface: "% Proxy Arp is not
                                             enabled in the interface"
      `alias` option, ARP behaviour ........ UNMEASURED (see CAVEAT)
      removal .............................. gone from running-config, gone from
                                             `show arp`, baseline restored on the wire
      reboot ............................... entry back, and 5 of 5 frames rewritten
                                             and forwarded again
CAVEAT:  The `alias` form of the same command (`arp A.B.C.D MAC alias`) is accepted,
         appears in running-config and is listed by `show arp` as static, but produced
         NO observable change in ARP replies in either configuration reachable on this
         bench: same-subnet, where the DUT stayed silent, and off-subnet, where plain
         `ip proxy-arp` already answered and the alias form is refused unless proxy ARP
         is also enabled on the owning interface. That limb is UNMEASURED; it wants an
         answer on the intended `alias` semantics rather than another bench leg. It is
         not the subject of this case and does not bear on the verdict. Everything else
         was exercised with one entry, one IPv4 /24 pair and one IFRANGE value, on a
         standalone unit; VRF forms were not exercised.

