  All 10 probes entered on eth3 addressed to the DUT's SVI MAC. On the far side the DUT
  emitted broadcast ARP requests for BOTH targets -- 3 retries each, sender 192.168.48.1
  with its own SVI MAC -- and forwarded no data frame at all. Unresolved means not
  forwarded. The DUT also ARPed back for the source 192.168.10.2 on eth3; nothing
  answers that, which is why no ICMP came back.

  => STEP 0 done: with no entry for either target the DUT forwards 0 of 10 frames and
     ARPs for both. This is the state every later leg is measured against, and it is
     what makes "5 of 5 forwarded" in STEP 2 a measurement rather than an assumption.

STEP 1 -- configure a static ARP entry and prove it is held as static       PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# arp 192.168.48.9 0011.2233.4499 port1.0.48
awplus(config)# end
awplus# show running-config | include arp
arp 192.168.48.9 0011.2233.4499 port1.0.48       <-- verbatim, as entered
awplus# show arp
IP Address      LL Address       Interface            Port        Type
192.168.48.9    0011.2233.4499   vlan48               port1.0.48  static

  Accepted with no `% ` line. `show arp` resolves the entry to the right interface --
  vlan48, worked out from the address rather than from the IFRANGE argument -- to the
  configured port, and types it `static`.

  => STEP 1 PASS: the command exists, is accepted, is held in running-config exactly as
     entered, and the resulting entry is typed `static` on the correct interface and
     port. TPS row 20 is satisfied on this build.

STEP 2 -- the entry is USED by the forwarding path, with a no-entry control in the same leg  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: this is the step `show arp` cannot answer -- a table read-back says an entry
  exists, not that the dataplane uses it. The same probes as STEP 0 are sent, with the
  control 192.168.48.10 (which has NO entry) in the SAME leg, same instrument.

  leg `L1b-static-on`:
  sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L1b-static-on --out <capture dir> \
    --iface eth3 --watch eth4 --probe 192.168.48.9,192.168.48.10 \
    --sender-ip 192.168.10.2 --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:01 \
    --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 5 --wait 5

LEG L1b-static-on  iface=eth3 watch=eth4 count=5
  sent total: 10 frame(s)
  cap L1b-static-on-eth3.pcap: 13 frame(s)
    x5     UDP  192.168.10.2    -> 192.168.48.9    :9 [ours]   [eth 00:11:22:33:44:01 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
    x5     UDP  192.168.10.2    -> 192.168.48.10   :9 [ours]   [eth 00:11:22:33:44:01 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
    x3     ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.2    tell 192.168.10.1      [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  cap L1b-static-on-eth4.pcap: 8 frame(s)
    x5     UDP  192.168.10.2    -> 192.168.48.9    :9 [ours]   [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:99]
    x3     ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.48.10   tell 192.168.48.1      [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
                                         <-- .9 forwarded and rewritten; .10 still ARPed for

  Against STEP 0's baseline, same instrument, same probes:
    - 5 of 5 frames for 192.168.48.9 EGRESSED on eth4, with Ethernet destination
      00:11:22:33:44:99 -- the exact MAC configured -- and Ethernet source
      e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43, the DUT's SVI MAC. The DUT performed the L2 rewrite from the
      static entry.
    - The DUT sent NO ARP request for 192.168.48.9 at all: 3 in the baseline, 0 now.
    - CONTROL, same leg: 192.168.48.10 has no entry. It was still ARPed for, 3 requests,
      and ZERO of its 5 frames were forwarded.
    - The tool's own send count (10) and the sending NIC's capture (10 marked frames)
      agree; the leg exited 0 with no counter disagreement.

  => STEP 2 PASS: the static entry is not merely stored, it is installed in the
     forwarding path -- it suppresses the DUT's ARP for that address and supplies the
     destination MAC of every routed frame, on the configured egress port. The control
     address in the same leg, differing only in having no entry, behaved exactly as the
     baseline did, so the change is attributable to the entry alone.

STEP 3 -- the `alias` option of the same command                            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: `arp A.B.C.D MAC alias` is offered by the CLI as "Respond to ARP requests for
  the IP address", so it is exercised as part of the command's surface. Baseline off
  first, then the dependency, then the wire.

  3a. Baseline, no alias entry (leg `L2a-alias-off`, --arp 192.168.10.77,192.168.10.78):
cap L2a-alias-off-eth3.pcap: 6 frame(s)
  x3  ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.77 tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x3  ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.78 tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
                                         <-- no replies, as expected with nothing configured

  3b. The first attempt to configure it was REFUSED:
awplus(config)# arp 192.168.10.77 0011.2233.4477 alias
% Proxy Arp is not enabled in the interface

      A documented dependency: the `alias` form requires `ip proxy-arp` on the interface
      that owns the aliased address's subnet. Confirmed twice -- a later attempt to
      alias 192.168.48.77 while proxy ARP was enabled on vlan10 but NOT on vlan48 was
      refused with the same message.

  3c. With the dependency satisfied on vlan10:
awplus(config)# interface vlan10
awplus(config-if)# ip proxy-arp
awplus(config-if)# exit
awplus(config)# arp 192.168.10.77 0011.2233.4477 alias
awplus(config)# end
awplus# show running-config | include arp
 ip proxy-arp
arp 192.168.10.77 0011.2233.4477  alias
arp 192.168.48.9 0011.2233.4499 port1.0.48
awplus# show arp
IP Address      LL Address       Interface            Port        Type
192.168.48.9    0011.2233.4499   vlan48               port1.0.48  static
192.168.10.77   0011.2233.4477   vlan10               -           static

  3d. On the wire, with the instrument control in the SAME leg (`L2d-selfarp-control`,
      --arp 192.168.10.1,192.168.10.77,192.168.10.78):
cap L2d-selfarp-control-eth3.pcap: 12 frame(s)
  x3  ARP-REQ    who-has 192.168.10.1  tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x3  ARP-REPLY  192.168.10.1  is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43    [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:01]
  x3  ARP-REQ    who-has 192.168.10.77 tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x3  ARP-REQ    who-has 192.168.10.78 tell 192.168.10.2  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
                                         <-- control answered 3/3; .77 and .78 silent

      The DUT answered 3 of 3 requests for its OWN address 192.168.10.1 with
      is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43. So the crafted request format is correct, the DUT is
      receiving and answering ARP on this interface, and the capture would have held a
      reply if one had been sent. The "no reply" below is a measurement, not an absent
      instrument. Measured: 192.168.10.77, with its alias entry installed and
      `ip proxy-arp` on vlan10, drew 0 replies in 3 requests; 192.168.10.78, with no
      entry, likewise 0. No difference.

  3e. The complementary leg (`L2e-alias-offsubnet`, --arp 192.168.48.77,192.168.48.5,
      192.168.10.77) shows what IS answering in this configuration:
cap L2e-alias-offsubnet-eth3.pcap: 15 frame(s)
  x3  ARP-REPLY  192.168.48.77 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:01]
  x3  ARP-REPLY  192.168.48.5  is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:01]
  (192.168.10.77: 3 requests, 0 replies)

      192.168.48.5 has NO alias entry and was answered just the same, so those replies
      are plain `ip proxy-arp` on vlan10 for addresses routed out vlan48, not the alias
      entry.

  => STEP 3 PASS for the command surface and its dependency: `arp A.B.C.D MAC alias` is
     present, is refused with a specific message naming the precondition when
     `ip proxy-arp` is absent from the owning interface, and once the precondition is
     met is accepted, held in running-config and listed by `show arp` as static on the
     right interface. The alias's effect on ARP replies is UNMEASURED: in both
     configurations reachable on this bench it produced no reply the DUT would not have
     produced anyway, with a live instrument control proving the measurement. That limb
     is recorded in the CAVEAT and is not the subject of this case.

